{
  "description": "Turning the phrases a user types into a filter pattern, and a saved pattern back into phrases. The saved filter still holds only `regex` (shared/patterns/README.md); this is how an editor fills it. `op` `build`: `phrases` as typed give `pattern`. Each phrase: (1) cut whitespace from both ends, where whitespace is exactly the pattern language's `\\s` set (U+0009-U+000D, U+0020, U+0085, U+00A0, U+1680, U+2000-U+200A, U+2028, U+2029, U+202F, U+205F, U+3000), and drop the phrase if nothing is left; (2) read it code point by code point: every run of whitespace becomes `\\s+`, each of `\\ ^ $ . | ? * + ( ) [ ] { }` gets a `\\` before it, and every other character stands for itself; (3) put `\\b` before it when its first character is a word character (`A-Z a-z 0-9 _`, the pattern language's `\\w`) and `\\b` after it when its last one is, so a phrase is found only as whole words while one that begins or ends with anything else (`C++`, `#shorts`, `café`) still matches. Drop a phrase whose result equals an earlier one's (so phrases differing only in their whitespace are one, and phrases differing in case are two), then join the results with `|`; no phrases give the empty pattern. Every result is a valid pattern. `canonical` is what `pattern` reads back as, and `matches` what it finds, as in shared/fixtures/patterns.json (shared/tools/cross-engine.ts runs these through all three engines too). `op` `parse`: `pattern` read back into `expected`, a list of phrases, or null when it is not phrases. Reading succeeds exactly for the patterns building can produce: split at each `|` that is not escaped, take off a leading and a trailing `\\b`, turn each `\\s+` into one space (U+0020) and each `\\` + one of the fourteen characters above into that character, and accept the list only if building it again gives the very same pattern; anything else (other escapes, an unescaped special character, a missing or extra `\\b`, a literal whitespace character, an empty alternative, a repeat) is not phrases, and an editor shows that pattern whole. Building what was read back gives the same pattern again.",
